---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';

// Sorted explicitly. "Whatever order the filesystem returned" is not an
// editorial decision, and it changes silently when a file is renamed.
const tools = (await getCollection('tools')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const extensions = (await getCollection('extensions')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<Base
  title="ubuos — tools for people who maintain things"
  description="Open-source maintainer tooling. Reads any repository, runs what needs running, remembers what you worked out."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="pill">Maintainer tooling</p>
    <h1>The context you already have,<br />assembled before you need it.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Reading a pull request is not the hard part. Remembering what you decided last time,
      whether it reproduces, and what the project expects — that is the hard part, and it is
      the part nothing keeps for you.
    </p>

    <div class="cta">
      <!-- Static-first: macOS is visible before any JS runs, so the frame that
           matters most never depends on a script. -->
      <div class="ostabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Install">
        <button class="ostab on" data-os="mac" role="tab" aria-selected="true"> macOS</button>
        <button class="ostab" data-os="linux" role="tab" aria-selected="false">🐧 Linux</button>
        <button class="ostab" data-os="win" role="tab" aria-selected="false">⊞ Windows</button>
      </div>
      <pre class="install" data-os="mac"><code>brew install ramanathan1504/oss-cli/oss</code></pre>
      <pre class="install hide" data-os="linux"><code>curl -LO https://github.com/ramanathan1504/oss-cli/releases/latest/download/oss_amd64.deb
sudo dpkg -i oss_amd64.deb</code></pre>
      <pre class="install hide" data-os="win"><code>choco install oss --source .\oss.nupkg   # from the release assets
wsl oss run …                             # packs run under WSL</code></pre>
      <p class="dim small">
        Self-contained — carries its own Java. A GitHub token is the whole requirement.
        No model, no server, no account.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="demo"><Terminal /></div>

    <div class="chips" aria-label="Commands">
      {['pr', 'review', 'followup', 'hub', 'pick', 'backlog', 'search', 'memory', 'run', 'model', 'backup'].map((c) => (
        <a class="chip" href="/docs/reference/commands/"><code>{c}</code></a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tools">
    <h2>The tool</h2>
    <p class="lede">One core. Everything else attaches to it.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      {tools.map((t) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h3>
            {t.data.name}
            {t.data.status !== 'stable' && <span class="pill status">{t.data.status}</span>}
          </h3>
          <p class="tagline">{t.data.tagline}</p>
          <p>{t.data.summary}</p>
          {t.data.install && <pre class="mini"><code>{t.data.install}</code></pre>}
          {t.data.repo && (
            <p class="more"><a href={t.data.repo}>Source →</a></p>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="more"><a href="/docs/connect/">Point it at your own project — build a pack →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="review">
    <h2>The loop it closes</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Reading a pull request is not the hard part. The parts that are — whether it
      reproduces, and what you decided last time — are the parts nothing keeps for you.
    </p>
    <div class="grid steps">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">1</span>
        <h3>Read the facts</h3>
        <p>Size, checks, files, who wrote it. <code>oss pr 4234 --repo owner/name</code></p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">2</span>
        <h3>Find what you knew</h3>
        <p>Related issues, prior fixes, your own notes — with no model needed.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">3</span>
        <h3>Make it run</h3>
        <p>Attach a runner and reproduce it for real, on the versions that matter.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">4</span>
        <h3>Know when it moves</h3>
        <p>
          <code>oss followup</code> records the head you reviewed, and tells you when the
          author pushes past it — across every project, in one list.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="more dim">
      A verdict on code that has since changed is worse than no verdict: it still
      looks like a review.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how">
    <h2>Nothing is mandatory</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      It works with none of it. Each layer only makes the next question cheaper.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>On its own</h3>
        <p>
          Facts about any repository, straight from the API. No clone, any language.
          Storing and filing work with no AI at all.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plus a runner</h3>
        <p>
          Answers <em>does this actually run?</em> — the one question a reader of code
          cannot answer by reading it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plus a memory</h3>
        <p>
          Answers <em>have I worked this out before?</em> Plain Markdown on your disk,
          filed by topic, readable in ten years.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plus a local model</h3>
        <p>
          Optional. Adds verdicts and search by meaning. Nothing is sent anywhere unless
          you ask for it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="extensions">
    <h2>Packs, and extensions</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      A <strong>pack</strong> is your playground: your applications, configurations and
      versions, described in one <code>pack.sh</code>. The engine that walks it ships
      inside <code>oss</code> — <code>oss run --pack ~/your-repo matrix</code> and nothing
      is registered. An <strong>extension</strong> is for a tool of your own that
      <code>oss</code> should call.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      {extensions.map((e) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h3>{e.data.name} <span class="pill">{e.data.kind}</span></h3>
          <p>{e.data.summary}</p>
          <pre class="mini"><code>{e.data.attach}</code></pre>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="more"><a href="/docs/connect/">Connect your project — pack or extension →</a></p>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    (() => {
      // Install tabs. Buttons swap which <pre> shows; static default is macOS.
      document.querySelectorAll('.ostab').forEach((b) => {
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          document.querySelectorAll('.ostab').forEach((x) => {
            x.classList.toggle('on', x === b);
            x.setAttribute('aria-selected', x === b ? 'true' : 'false');
          });
          document.querySelectorAll('.install').forEach((p) => {
            p.classList.toggle('hide', p.dataset.os !== b.dataset.os);
          });
        });
      });

      // Sections rise in as they enter the viewport. Content is fully visible
      // without JS or with reduced motion -- the animation is seasoning, never
      // the meal.
      if (matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return;
      // threshold 0: any visible pixel reveals the section. A fraction threshold
      // can leave a tall section invisible on a short viewport — content held at
      // opacity 0 reads as a page full of empty space.
      const io = new IntersectionObserver((es) => {
        es.forEach((e) => { if (e.isIntersecting) { e.target.classList.add('in'); io.unobserve(e.target); } });
      }, { threshold: 0 });
      document.querySelectorAll('section').forEach((sec, i) => {
        if (i === 0) return;               // the hero is already on screen
        sec.classList.add('reveal');
        io.observe(sec);
      });
    })();
  </script>

  <style>
    .hero { padding-top: clamp(28px, 4.5vw, 52px); }
    .demo { margin-top: 24px; }
    .cta { margin-top: 20px; max-width: 620px; }
    .small { font-size: 14px; margin-top: 10px; }
    .tagline { color: var(--fg) !important; font-weight: 520; margin-bottom: 6px !important; }
    .mini { margin-top: 11px; font-size: 12.5px; }
    .more { margin-top: 12px; }
    .more a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; font-weight: 520; }
    .more a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    .status { margin-left: 8px; }
    .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
    .steps .pill { margin-bottom: 8px; }
    @media (max-width: 900px) { .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
    @media (max-width: 560px) { .steps { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
    .steps h3 { margin-top: 2px; }

    .ostabs { display: flex; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    .ostab {
      font: inherit; font-size: 13px; padding: 6px 13px;
      border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 999px;
      background: transparent; color: var(--muted); cursor: pointer;
      transition: color .15s, border-color .15s, background .15s;
    }
    .ostab:hover { color: var(--fg); }
    .ostab.on { color: var(--fg); border-color: var(--accent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 9%, transparent); }
    .install.hide { display: none; }

    .chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-top: 16px; }
    .chip {
      text-decoration: none; font-size: 12.5px;
      padding: 5px 11px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 999px;
      color: var(--muted); background: var(--card);
      transition: transform .15s, color .15s, border-color .15s;
    }
    .chip:hover { color: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); transform: translateY(-1px); }
    .chip code { background: none; padding: 0; font-size: inherit; color: inherit; }

    .reveal { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(18px); transition: opacity .55s ease, transform .55s ease; }
    .reveal.in { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  </style>
</Base>
